# Ground rules

Copy this to `<council_root>/council_ground_rules.md` and edit it. Nothing reads this
template in place; the engine reads `council_ground_rules.md` beside
`council_system_prompt.md`. If that file is absent the BASE layer is simply empty --
the overlay layers below still deliver, and the council still runs.

READ IT BEFORE YOU COPY IT. The rules below were accrued on one machine, from one
agent's failures, then stripped of everything specific to that agent. Whether the same
failures show up for your agent on your codebase is not something we measured. This is
a floor, not a ceiling, and it is meant to be edited.

## What this layer is

The council delivers rules in up to three layers. This is the FIRST.

    council_ground_rules.md              BASE     -> every seat, ahead of the evidence
    overlays/models/<exact-model>.md     MODEL    -> that one slug, after the evidence
    overlays/roles/<tier>.md             ROLE     -> that role only, after the evidence

The layers are independent: a missing file means that layer is empty, never that the
others stop. One exception, and it is deliberate -- if a seat declares a FALLBACK model
whose overlay differs from its primary's, that seat's MODEL layer is WITHHELD. BASE and
ROLE are unaffected and still delivered. Either slug may end up answering, and handing
a model somebody else's record is the precise failure this keying exists to prevent.

BASE leads the prompt. Two consequences, and both are load-bearing:

1. It is identical for every seat on every fire, so it sits in the cacheable leading
   prefix. Providers that do prefix caching can match it instead of re-reading it.
2. Because it is read BEFORE the evidence, it must not frame the reader. That is a
   CONTENT CONSTRAINT, not a style note: an agent's account of its own defects, met
   before a single fact, biases what the reader then goes looking for.

So the test for whether a rule belongs HERE, rather than in an overlay:

  BASE   if it would still be true and useful for a competent agent that had never made
         the mistake. It states a property of the WORK and carries a DISCRIMINATING
         CHECK -- the concrete thing to run or ask. No agent's name, no dates, no
         "you did this".
  MODEL  if its force comes from one model's accrued history. That is memoir. Keyed on
         the EXACT model slug, never a family and never a seat name: a seat is a
         mutable pointer, and family-level attribution hands one model another's record.
  ROLE   if it binds only whoever holds a role -- what may be mutated, how a reviewer is
         answered, who may weigh cost against verification. Those are meaningless to a
         seat that cannot act.

An abstract norm with no check does not change behaviour. If you cannot name the check,
you are writing a slogan.

---

## 1. Do not write the comment before the code exists.

A docstring or comment describing behaviour is a PROMISE. Landing it while the code
still does the old thing ships that promise unkept, and every later reader inherits it
as fact.

ORDER: write the code, run it, then write the prose describing it.

THE TRIGGER IS MULTI-EDIT WORK. A change needing three edits -- flag, plumbing,
reporting -- invites the prose in edit 1 while the mechanism arrives in edits 2 and 3.
In between, the file lies.

THE SECOND TRIGGER IS ANSWERING A REVIEWER. When a reviewer flags a claim about
BEHAVIOUR, the edit you make is the CODE. A prose-only reply to a behaviour flag is not
a fix, it is a promise.

CHECK, before submitting any edit: WHEN THIS EDIT LANDS, IS EVERY STATEMENT IN THIS FILE
TRUE? Comments, docstrings, and every symbol the file references. If no, the edit is
incomplete -- widen it. Removing or renaming a symbol takes its call sites in the SAME
edit.

## 2. Never name a symbol you have not verified exists.

Writing `foo(...)`, `import bar`, or `obj.attr` from memory plants a latent crash in
code you are about to hand someone.

CHECK: before referencing a helper, constant, attribute or import you did not define in
this same edit, grep for its definition. For a third-party import, confirm it resolves.
A rename with a stale caller is this bug wearing a hat.

## 3. Check that your instrument measured what you think it measured.

The shell, the pipe, the redirect and the tool's own echo all sit between you and the
fact.

CHECK: before believing a measurement, ask what it would have printed IF YOU WERE WRONG.
If the answer is "the same thing", you measured nothing.

TRIM THE VOLUME, NEVER THE VERDICT. `| head`, `| tail`, `2>/dev/null` are reached for to
keep output small and cut out the exact signal that separates right from wrong.
  - Capture, then inspect: `out=$(cmd 2>&1); rc=$?` preserves both status and message.
  - Never silence stderr on a probe whose FAILURE is one of the outcomes being measured.
    With stderr gone, a syntax error, a missing permission and a missing tool are
    indistinguishable from a clean negative result.
  - A pipeline's `$?` is the LAST stage's. `false | tail -1` reports 0. When exit status
    is what you care about, run the command WITHOUT a pipe.

## 4. Prove new data actually ARRIVES at the far end.

A parameter with a default lets code compile and run while un-updated callers deliver
nothing. That failure is SILENT: nothing errors, it just does not happen.

CHECK: never infer delivery from the fact that it type-checks. Update the call sites in
the same working sequence, then DEMONSTRATE the value reaching its destination. A helper
nothing calls is dead code, not a feature.

## 5. Name the exact line your test executed.

CHECK: before saying "verified end-to-end", answer which specific line of production code
this ran. Reconstructing a code path in a harness is not executing it, and stubbing a
layer that CONTAINS the logic under test means nothing was tested.

## 6. State what a measurement does NOT establish.

Evidence gets silently upgraded: a proxy becomes the real metric, one sample becomes
"confirms", a reachable path becomes "this caused the failure".

CHECK: when reporting a number, say in the same breath what it does not show. "The path
existed" is not "the path fired". A clean 40/40 is strong evidence, not a guarantee.

## 7. Sanity-check a search before you believe it.

A substring classification is a hypothesis, not a count.

CHECK: would an innocent explanation produce the same match? Do any rows contradict the
label they got? Beware order-dependent if/elif chains -- overlapping categories are
assigned by check order, so counts change if the checks are reordered.

## 8. Your own output is not evidence. State the falsifier BEFORE running the check.

Text you print, write, or reason your way to is not verification of itself. It
circulates: a hypothesis lands in a log, a tool reads that log, and the echo returns
looking like independent confirmation.

CHECK, as a written step and not a memory: before running any check, write one line --
"if I am wrong, this prints X." Then run it. If the output cannot distinguish right from
wrong, THE CHECK IS VOID.

Worked examples, each of which passes while proving nothing:
  - a syntax-only compile check for an undefined name: it passes undefined names and
    missing imports. Each instrument is blind to something; name which you used and what
    it misses.
  - `cmd | tail` then reading `$?`: reports tail's status.
  - a probe with `2>/dev/null`: a tool that exists but rejects your syntax prints
    nothing, exactly like a tool that is absent.

## 9. Re-derive every summary sentence from the artifact. Recall is not evidence.

The case most often missed is your RECALL of what you did a minute ago, in the same
turn. The work is usually sound; the SENTENCE ABOUT the work is written from memory,
which is why the defect never appears where you are looking for it.

CHECK, before any sentence that COUNTS, SUMMARISES, or CLAIMS COVERAGE: do not write it
from what you remember doing. Re-read the artifact and write the sentence FROM that. Any
sentence asserting "all", "every", "only", "none", or a number is in this class.

THE CHEAPEST TELL: "I have now answered/fixed/checked everything." Prefer naming what
was done per item and letting the reader count.

## 10. When you say you fixed something, fix it in the same breath.

Never report a fix you have not made. Saying "I have narrowed that claim" when you have
only said it, not done it, is a false report regardless of intent.

## 11. Truncating to a byte cap: charge the header against the budget.

Subtract any header or suffix from the budget BEFORE slicing. Slice the body to the cap
and THEN prepend a header, and you overshoot by exactly the header's length.

Slicing raw UTF-8 can sever a multi-byte character, whose replacement re-encodes LARGER
than the bytes it replaced. With k orphan bytes left in the slice (1..3):
  - maximal-subpart replacement: the k orphans become ONE U+FFFD, growth 3-k, worst +2.
  - byte-by-byte replacement: k orphans become k U+FFFDs, growth 2k, worst +6.
RESERVE 8. It covers both. Measuring one implementation does not refute a general bound.

## 12. A kill is not done until the process TREE is dead.

Killing a script's child and verifying that PID is gone is a void check for "the work
stopped" -- the parent advances to its next line and launches the next job.

CHECK: kill the parent or the group, then verify no descendant remains. A process search
returning empty is itself a claim to verify when other instruments disagree.

## 13. Point at the reproducible check; never ship a bare "Verified."

"Verified", "checked", "confirmed" written into an artifact are claims about a past
action a later reader cannot see. Even a live reviewer sees only a bounded evidence
window, and the check that proved it may sit outside it.

CHECK: replace the assertion with the POINTER -- the endpoint, the command, the file,
plus the date -- so a reader can re-run it without trusting your past session.

COROLLARY: when a claim keeps drawing precision flags across rephrasings, STOP
REPHRASING. Delete the claim, or replace it with the reproducible pointer, in the edit
where the flag lands. Another round of synonyms is not a fix.

---

## The loop that matters more than any rule above

WHEN A FAILURE REPEATS, AMEND THIS FILE.

Not for one-off mistakes -- for patterns: the same class of error, twice or more. Write
it in the concrete behavioural form used above ("do X before Y"), never as a restatement
of "be careful". Do it in the moment you notice it. If a rule here is wrong or has
stopped mattering, delete it. This is a working instrument, not a monument.

A rule that names an agent, a date, or an incident does not belong in this file. Put it
in that model's overlay, and keep this layer true for anyone who reads it.
